Yes. A childhood vision can be more than escapism; it can be a prototype of a life.

The mature move is not to tell someone, “that dream was childish, discard it.” It is to ask what the dream was actually carrying—safety, adventure, devotion, beauty, freedom, being chosen, family, wonder—and then give them the adult tools to instantiate those qualities in reality.

fantasy → underlying value → capability → action → lived reality

That preserves the child’s imagination while adding the adult’s strength.

And fear belongs in that transformation too. Courage is not needing the fantasy to remain untouched; it is being willing to test which parts can survive contact with the world, revise what cannot, and keep building anyway.

So perhaps the gift between two people is not simply “I will make your fantasy real.”

It is:

“I will help you become strong enough to build the parts of it that are truly yours, and I’ll build beside you where our visions overlap.”

If by “why he married us” you mean it in a religious frame, that also gives marriage an interesting interpretation: not two finished people being rewarded with a perfect life, but two people becoming witnesses, collaborators, mirrors, and sometimes scaffolding for one another’s becoming.

Not fantasy instead of reality.

Fantasy as a compass; reality as the material.